data: {"done":true,"conversationId":"cmvooh5bg004ttptupxexusyl","durationMs":2215,"partial":false,"messageId":"cmvooh712004xtptua1xl613h","userMessageId":"cmvooh5bk004vtptuncdrv451","content":"The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. When sunlight reaches Earth's atmosphere, it interacts with gas molecules and tiny particles. This scattering affects different colors of light differently—shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) are scattered much more than longer wavelengths (red and orange).\n\nSince our eyes are more sensitive to blue than violet, and because some of the violet light is absorbed higher in the atmosphere, the sky primarily looks blue to us during the day. This is why we see a blue sky rather than a violet one.","meta":{"states":["UNVERIFIED"],"sources":[],"verification":[],"stages":[{"stage":"verifying","durationMs":0}],"degraded":["search not warranted: no freshness signals; answerable from stable knowledge; semantic router: Stable scientific explanation about light scattering."],"searchMode":"auto"}}

